《Biographies of 張釋之 and 馮唐》

張廷尉釋之 was a man of 堵陽, courtesy name 季. He had an elder brother 仲 with whom he lived. Because of his wealth he became a Cavalry Gentleman, serving 孝文帝; for ten years he received no promotion and was not known by name. 釋之 said: "Long service is diminishing 仲's property, and I have not succeeded." He wished to resign and return home. 中郎將 袁盎 knew he was worthy and regretted his departure, so he requested that 釋之 be transferred to fill the post of 謁者. After 釋之 had finished court audience, he stepped forward and spoke about matters of practical benefit. 文帝 said: "Keep it low; do not make lofty arguments, but let it be something that can be put into practice now." Thereupon 釋之 spoke of affairs between 秦 and 漢, of why 秦 lost and why 漢 rose, for a long time. 文帝 praised him, and then appointed 釋之 as 謁者仆射.

釋之 accompanied the emperor on a tour and ascended the tiger enclosure. The emperor asked the 上林尉 about the registers of the birds and beasts; he asked more than ten questions, and the 尉 looked left and right, completely unable to answer. The tiger enclosure 嗇夫, from the side, answered on behalf of the 尉 the questions the emperor asked about the registers of birds and beasts very thoroughly, wishing to display his ability to respond fluently without end. 文帝 said: "Should not an official be like this? The 尉 is good for nothing!" He then ordered 釋之 to appoint the 嗇夫 as 上林令. After a long while 釋之 stepped forward and said: "Your Majesty, what sort of man do you consider 絳侯 周勃 to be?" The emperor said: "A senior man." He asked again: "What sort of man do you consider 東陽侯 張相如 to be?" The emperor again said: "A senior man." 釋之 said: "Now 絳侯 and 東陽侯 are called senior men; these two men, when speaking of affairs, could not even get the words out of their mouths. How could they imitate this 嗇夫's glib, sharp-tongued, quick-witted eloquence! Moreover, 秦 employed clerks who wielded the knife and brush; the officials competed to be superior in urgency, severity, and harsh scrutiny, but their defect was merely formal documents, without the substance of compassion. For this reason they did not hear of their own faults, and decline continued until it reached 二世, when the empire collapsed like a landslide. Now Your Majesty, because of this 嗇夫's eloquence, promotes him beyond his rank; I fear the empire will follow the wind and sway, competing in eloquence without substance. Moreover, the transformation of those below by those above is faster than shadow and echo; in action and decision one cannot but be careful." 文帝 said: "Good." He then stopped and did not appoint the 嗇夫.

The emperor mounted his carriage, summoned 釋之 to ride beside him, and proceeded slowly, asking 釋之 about the defects of 秦. He answered fully and plainly. When they reached the palace, the emperor appointed 釋之 as 公車令.

Shortly afterward, the 太子 and the 梁王 rode together into court and did not dismount at the 司馬門; thereupon 釋之 pursued and stopped the 太子 and the 梁王, not allowing them to enter the palace gate. He then impeached them for not dismounting at the public gate, for disrespect, and submitted the memorial. 薄太后 heard of it; 文帝 removed his cap and apologized, saying: "I did not teach my sons carefully." 薄太后 then sent an envoy to convey a decree pardoning the 太子 and the 梁王, after which they were allowed to enter. From this 文帝 regarded 釋之 as extraordinary and appointed him 中大夫.

Shortly afterward, he reached the post of 中郎將. He accompanied the emperor on a tour to 霸陵, and stayed at the northern side facing the latrine. At that time 慎夫人 accompanied them; the emperor pointed out to 慎夫人 the road to 新豐, saying: "This is the road to 邯鄲." He had 慎夫人 play the se zither, and the emperor himself leaned on the se zither and sang; his mood was sorrowful and mournful, and he looked back at the assembled ministers and said: "Alas! If one uses the stones of the northern mountains as an outer coffin, uses hemp floss and lacquer to fill the gaps, and lacquers the spaces between, how could it be moved!" Those around all said: "Good." 釋之 stepped forward and said: "If there is something desirable inside, even if it is sealed in the southern mountains there will still be a crack; if there is nothing desirable inside, even without a stone outer coffin, what grief is there!" 文帝 praised him. Afterward he appointed 釋之 as 廷尉.

Shortly afterward, the emperor went out on a tour and crossed the 中渭橋; a man ran out from under the bridge, and the horses of the imperial carriage were startled. Thereupon he sent cavalry to arrest him and handed him over to the 廷尉. 釋之 interrogated him. He said: "I am a man from the county; I came, heard the clearing of the way, and hid under the bridge. After a long while, thinking the procession had already passed, I came out, saw the imperial carriage and cavalry, and immediately ran." The 廷尉 秦 law judged: one man violated the clearing of the way; the appropriate punishment was a fine of gold. 文帝 was angry and said: "This man personally startled my horses; my horses happened to be gentle and docile—if it had been other horses, would they not certainly have injured me? Yet the 廷尉 judges him only a fine of gold!" 釋之 said: "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares in common with the empire. Now if the law is thus and yet it is made heavier, then the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at the time, if Your Majesty had ordered him executed on the spot, that would have been the end of it. Now that he has already been handed down to the 廷尉, the 廷尉 is the balance of the empire; once it tilts, the whole empire in applying the law will all become light or heavy, and the people will have nowhere to place their hands and feet. I beg Your Majesty to examine this." After a long while, the emperor said: "The 廷尉's judgment is correct."

Afterward someone stole the jade ring in front of the seat in the 高廟; he was caught, and 文帝 was angry and handed him down to the 廷尉 for judgment. 釋之 examined the statutes concerning those who steal objects used in the ancestral temples and submitted a memorial; the memorial judged that he should be executed in the marketplace. The emperor was greatly angry and said: "This man is without the Way; he even stole the vessels of the former emperor's temple. I handed him to the 廷尉 because I wished to have him executed along with his clan, but you judge him according to the law and submit it—this is not how I intend to jointly serve the ancestral temples." 釋之 removed his cap, knocked his head on the ground, and apologized, saying: "The law is sufficient as it is. Moreover, the crimes are equal, but they are differentiated according to rebellion and obedience. Now if one steals temple vessels and his clan is executed, suppose there were one chance in ten thousand that, for example, a foolish commoner took a handful of earth from 長陵—what would Your Majesty add to his punishment?" After a long while, 文帝 spoke with 太后 about it, and then approved the 廷尉's judgment. At that time, 中尉 條侯 周亞夫 and the 梁相 山都侯 王恬開 saw that 釋之 held to impartial judgment, and so they became close friends. 張廷尉 was from this praised throughout the empire.

Later 文帝 died, 景帝 ascended the throne, and 釋之 was afraid; he claimed illness. He wished to resign and leave, but feared that great punishment would come; he wished to appear and apologize, but did not know how it would turn out. Using 王生's plan, he finally appeared and apologized, and 景帝 did not blame him.

王生 was one who was good at the words of 黃老; he was a recluse. He was once summoned to reside in the court; the 三公 and 九卿 all assembled and stood. 王生 was an old man, and said, "My shoe has come loose," and turned to 張廷尉: "Tie my shoe for me!" 釋之 knelt and tied it. After it was done, someone said to 王生: "Why on earth did you humiliate 張廷尉 in court, making him kneel to tie your shoe?" 王生 said: "I am old and lowly, and I estimate that in the end I can be of no benefit to 張廷尉. 張廷尉 is now a famous minister of the empire; I therefore deliberately humiliated the 廷尉 a little, making him kneel to tie my shoe, wishing thereby to give him weight." The gentlemen heard this, considered 王生 worthy, and gave greater weight to 張廷尉.

張廷尉 served 景帝 for a little over a year, and became 相 of 淮南王, still because of his former fault. After a long while, 釋之 died. His son was called 張摯, courtesy name 長公; he reached the office of 大夫, and was dismissed. Because he could not ingratiate himself with the age, he remained unemployed for life.

馮唐: his grandfather was a man of 趙. His father moved to 代. When 漢 arose, he moved to 安陵. 唐 was noted for filial piety, and became 中郎署長, serving 文帝. 文帝 passed by in his carriage and asked 唐: "Old sir, why are you yourself a 郎? Where is your home?" 唐 answered fully and truthfully. 文帝 said: "When I lived in 代, my 尚食監 高袪 often spoke to me of the worthiness of the 趙 general 李齊, who fought below 鉅鹿. Now whenever I eat, my thoughts are never not on 鉅鹿. Does the old sir know of him?" 唐 replied: "He is still not as good as 廉頗 and 李牧 as generals." The emperor said: "How so?" 唐 said: "My grandfather, when he was in 趙, was a commanding general, and was friendly with 李牧. My father formerly served as 相 of 代, and was friendly with the 趙 general 李齊, and knew his character." After the emperor had heard about the character of 廉頗 and 李牧, he was greatly pleased, and slapped his thigh and said: "Alas! If only I could have had 廉頗 and 李牧 as my generals in my time, would I worry about the 匈奴!" 唐 said: "Lord! Even if Your Majesty obtained 廉頗 and 李牧, you would not be able to use them." The emperor was angry, rose, and entered the forbidden palace. After a long while, he summoned 唐 and reproached him, saying: "How could you publicly humiliate me? Is there no private place?" 唐 apologized, saying: "This rustic does not know taboos."

At that time, the 匈奴 had recently made a great incursion into 朝那, killing the 北地都尉 卬. The emperor was concerned about the 胡 raids, and so finally again asked 唐: "How do you know that I cannot use 廉頗 and 李牧?" 唐 replied: "I have heard that when the kings of high antiquity sent out a general, they knelt and pushed his carriage, saying: 'Within the gate, I control it; beyond the gate, the general controls it.' Military merit, rank, and reward are all decided outside, and upon return they are reported. This is not empty talk. My grandfather said that when 李牧 was a 趙 general stationed on the frontier, the taxes from the military market were all used by him to feast the soldiers; rewards and punishments were decided outside, and were not disturbed from within. He was entrusted with responsibility and held accountable for success; therefore 李牧 was able to fully use his intelligence and ability, dispatching a thousand three hundred selected chariots, thirteen thousand 彀騎, and a hundred thousand men of a hundred gold, and so in the north he drove back the 單于, broke the 東胡, destroyed 澹林, in the west he checked the strong 秦, and in the south he supported 韓 and 魏. At that time, 趙 almost became hegemon. Afterward it happened that 趙王 遷 ascended the throne; his mother was a singer. When 王 遷 ascended the throne, he then used the slander of 郭開, and finally executed 李牧, ordering 顏聚 to replace him. Therefore the army was broken and the soldiers routed, and he was captured and destroyed by 秦. Now I have privately heard that 魏尚 is 雲中守; he uses all the taxes from the military market to feast the soldiers, and [out of] his private funds for supporting them, every five days he slaughters an ox, feasting guests, military officers, and retainers; therefore the 匈奴 keep far away and do not approach the passes of 雲中. The caitiffs once made one incursion, and 尚 led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many of them. Now the soldiers are all sons of common families, rising from the fields to join the army; how would they know the registers of service and the tallies of five? All day they fight hard, beheading and capturing prisoners, and report their merits to the headquarters; if one word does not correspond, the clerks bind them with the law. Their rewards are not carried out, while the officials' enforcement of the law is necessarily applied. I, being foolish, consider that Your Majesty's laws are too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, 雲中守 魏尚 was convicted because the number of heads and captives reported for merit differed by six grades; Your Majesty handed him to the officials, stripped his rank, and punished him with penal labor. From this it may be said that even if Your Majesty obtained 廉頗 and 李牧, you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish, and have offended against taboos; death penalty, death penalty!" 文帝 was pleased. On that day he ordered 馮唐 to hold the tally and pardon 魏尚, and reinstate him as 雲中守, and appointed 唐 as 車騎都尉, in charge of the 中尉 and the chariot soldiers of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, 景帝 ascended the throne, and appointed 唐 as 相 of 楚, and then dismissed him. 武帝 ascended the throne, sought worthy and good men, and recommended 馮唐. 唐 was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve in office, so he made 唐's son 馮遂 a 郎. 遂's courtesy name was 王孫; he too was an extraordinary man, and was friendly with me.

The Grand Historian says: 張季's words about senior men—he upheld the law and did not flatter; 馮公's discussion of generals—how flavorful! How flavorful! The saying goes, "If you do not know the man, look at his friends." What these two gentlemen praised can be displayed in the ancestral temple. The Book says, "Not partial, not partisan—the kingly way is broad and level; not partisan, not partial—the kingly way is smooth and easy." 張季 and 馮公 come close to this. [end of text]


